WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.950
- [Harry] From John Wick to John Wayne,

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:23.390
gun fights in movies
can often look so real

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:25.890
that it's hard to imagine
how they're put together.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:27.780
Here at the British Action Academy,

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.300
stuntman Andreas Petrides teaches students

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.633
how to safely perform firearm sequences.

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:43.800
[Andreas] Holding it, 10-point
check. One, two, three.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.210
We do gun rush today,

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.010
which is the Firearms for Films course,

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:48.970
which teaches people
about the performance side

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:51.210
of using firearms on a film set.

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:54.020
My background, I've been a
stuntman for about 28 years now.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.500
I've worked on many, many productions.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:58.180
"Star Wars," I doubled Ewan McGregor

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:01.250
in all the lightsaber fights
and obviously trained Ewan.

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:02.560
On "Bond" films, I've done both.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.660
I've trained the actors, and also

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:08.300
I've played characters myself,
and I've doubled characters.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:09.870
- [Harry] In the old days, of course,

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.170
people used to actually get shot.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:16.993
Nowadays, though, films use a
series of dummy replacements.

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:18.300
There are blanks, real working weapons

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.250
loaded with blank cartridges.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:23.280
These are for when you just need

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.010
a big muzzle flash or a loud bang.

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:28.600
'Function' guns are replica
guns that don't actually fire.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:30.720
Or for cheaper fake versions

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.530
that don't look as real in a close-up,

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:35.293
some movies use rubber guns.

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:37.660
The person responsible for these on a set?

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:40.080
The Weapons Master. And stunt performers

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.930
need to know exactly how to use them.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.960
The thing with blanks is that

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:49.063
they do still make a lot of noise,

00:01:49.063 --> 00:01:50.500
and you get the kind of flash as well

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:52.840
that you expect from a gunshot.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:54.740
So it's a really realistic experience.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:01:59.630
For actors, it's all about

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:01.464
selling the idea that it's a real gun.

00:02:32.871 --> 00:02:33.704
Action!

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.140
[Participant] Bang bang.

00:02:56.128 --> 00:02:58.927
Bang! Bang!

00:02:58.927 --> 00:02:59.830
[Harry] And of course,

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:01.390
reacting to being shot is important.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.010
[Harry] They teach you the
ways to kind of roll onto your

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:41.740
body so that you don't
just smack the floor,

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:43.760
but it does still kind of hurt.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.710
These guys, they don't mess about.

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:47.210
One thing that can be very real

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:50.200
is the weight of the equipment
actors have to lug around.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.350
According to Andreas, actors on the set

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:55.670
of "Saving Private Ryan" wore
packs weighted down with ammo,

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:58.320
all in the name of believability.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:10.720
The prop guns themselves go
hand in hand with sound design.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:13.860
When a gun fires, you're
hearing three acoustic elements:

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:17.000
the muzzle blast sound, the impact point,

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.080
and the crack sound of the
bullet traveling through the air.

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:22.210
Without this layering,

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:24.160
a movie just doesn't seem as realistic.

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:30.063
Cinema is filled with basic gun mistakes.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:47.030
So how would a beginner
go about getting it right?

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:50.134
[Andreas] My advice to anybody
who wants to get into the

00:04:50.134 --> 00:04:54.020
industry as a stunt performer
is, it's dedication.

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:55.180
You know, it's part of your life,

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:57.180
you gotta really want to do it.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:58.330
It's not a part-time
hobby, and the more skills

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:01.457
you have under your belt,
the more employable you are.

